version: 3
metadata:
  name: basic_nav_roqsim
  title: Basic nav2 navigation on the roqsim (MuJoCo) TurtleBot 4 (Depot)
  description: |
    A TurtleBot 4 navigates the nav2 Depot world to a per-configuration goal with nav2 + AMCL, in
    roqsim (MuJoCo). The roqsim half of the basic_nav pair: same map and the same measurement half
    as basic_nav_gazebo.vast. The bridge publishes the same ROS graph Gazebo does (incl. a
    ground-truth turtlebot4_base_link_gt frame), so roqsim is a drop-in replacement.

    Three containers, which is what this file is really about. `simulation` is roqsim, started by
    the backend from its own image -- nothing here installs a simulator. `sut` is a nav2 image kept
    vanilla: RoboVAST layers scenario_execution_server onto it and the scenario drives its launch
    over remote("ipc:///ipc/sut"). `scenario` is the stock RoboVAST image, so this campaign builds
    no image of its own.

    Two channels are varied. `scenario:` carries the goal, `sut:` rewrites nav2's own parameter
    file per configuration -- 5 goals x 2 inflation radii.
  keywords: [robotics, navigation, ROS2, mujoco, roqsim, nav2, depot]
configuration:
- name: goal
  variations:
  # Map-frame goals; the robot starts at the map origin. Identical to basic_nav_gazebo.vast.
  - ParameterVariationList:
      scenario: goal_pose
      values:
      - position: {x: 21.0, y: 6.0}
      - position: {x: 21.0, y: 4.0}
      - position: {x: 21.0, y: -2.0}
      - position: {x: 21.0, y: -4.0}
      - position: {x: 21.0, y: -6.0}
  # Keys in the stack's own file, addressed through the source `nav2` declared below. Both
  # costmaps in one factor: destinations pair with values POSITIONALLY, so each level is a pair.
  - ParameterVariationList:
      sut:
      - nav2.global_costmap.global_costmap.ros__parameters.inflation_layer.inflation_radius
      - nav2.local_costmap.local_costmap.ros__parameters.inflation_layer.inflation_radius
      values: [[0.30, 0.30], [0.55, 0.55]]
  # Arms the rewrite that hands each cell its own copy: a parameter left at its .osc default
  # is not one the campaign set, so the trial would launch against the unstaged original.
  - ParameterVariationList:
      scenario: params_file
      values: ['files/nav2_params.yaml']
visualization:
  # The Config tab's third column: what each generated configuration contains.
  config:
    panels:
    - parameters: {title: Scenario parameters, height: "35%"}
    - world: {title: World configuration, height: "15%"}
    # `map2d` and not `scene3d`: the 3D panel keys its geometry on the simulator image, which
    # the config view has no campaign to resolve. The 2D map is what nav2 plans in anyway.
    - map2d:
        title: Map
        # The very map the scenario hands nav2, so this is the grid the run plans on.
        map: files/depot.yaml
        markers:
        # MAP-frame -- this panel *is* the map -- so goal_pose needs no offset. The robot
        # spawns at world (-8, 0) and map = world + (8, 0), where AMCL is seeded.
        - {kind: pose, pos: [0.0, 0.0], yaw: 0.0, label: start, color: "#60a5fa"}
        - {kind: pose, param: goal_pose, label: goal, color: "#4ade80"}
  results:
    # No `scene3d` entry: the roqsim backend contributes it.
    run_view:
      panels:
      # Two trees answering different questions: what this trial did, and why nav2 recovered.
      # Half the column each; both claiming `left` would stack them on top of each other.
      - nav2_behavior_tree:
          position: {anchor: top-left, width: 320, height: "calc(50% - 44px)"}
          minimizable: true
      - scenario_tree:
          title: Scenario
          position: {anchor: bottom-left, width: 320, height: "calc(50% - 44px)"}
          minimizable: true
          source: {table: behaviors}
      - costmap:
          title: Nav2 costmaps
          position: {anchor: top-right, width: 320, height: 550}
          minimizable: true
          layers:
            map: {topic: /map}
            global: {topic: /global_costmap/costmap}
            local: {topic: /local_costmap/costmap}
            poses: {table: poses}
      # The declared width is what floats `bottom-center` above the playback bar.
      - log:
          title: Run log
          position: {anchor: bottom-center, width: "calc(100% - 680px)", height: 380}
          minimizable: true
          minimized: true
      # `vega` and not `timeseries` because speed is derived: the run records poses, not
      # velocities. `datum['position.x']` rather than a `field`: the columns contain dots.
      - vega:
          title: base_link
          position: {anchor: bottom-right, width: 320, height: 380}
          minimizable: true
          # `decimate_hz` because TF outruns the query's row cap, and that cap cuts at the
          # HEAD: without it the chart stops mid-run while looking finished.
          source: {table: poses, filter: {frame: base_link}, decimate_hz: 5}
          vega_lite:
            resolve: {scale: {x: shared}}
            spacing: 8
            transform:
            - {calculate: "datum['position.x']", as: px}
            - {calculate: "datum['position.y']", as: py}
            - {calculate: "datum['orientation.yaw']", as: yaw}
            - window:
              - {op: lag, field: px, as: px0}
              - {op: lag, field: py, as: py0}
              - {op: lag, field: timestamp, as: t0}
              sort: [{field: timestamp}]
            - filter: "isValid(datum.t0)"
            # Dropped, not clamped: two TF samples can share a receive time, and max(dt, 1e-6)
            # would turn such a pair into a 4000 m/s spike.
            - filter: "datum.timestamp - datum.t0 > 0"
            - calculate: >
                sqrt(pow(datum.px - datum.px0, 2) + pow(datum.py - datum.py0, 2))
                / (datum.timestamp - datum.t0)
              as: speed
            - window: [{op: mean, field: speed, as: speed_avg}]
              frame: [-9, 0]
              sort: [{field: timestamp}]
            vconcat:
            - height: 150
              encoding:
                x: {field: timestamp, type: quantitative, axis: null}
                y: {field: speed_avg, type: quantitative, title: "speed [m/s]"}
              layer:
              # The faint raw line keeps visible what the rolling mean smoothed away.
              - mark: {type: line, opacity: 0.25, strokeWidth: 1}
                encoding: {y: {field: speed, type: quantitative}}
              - mark: {type: line, strokeWidth: 1.5}
            - height: 120
              transform: [{fold: [px, py, yaw], as: [series, value]}]
              mark: {type: line, strokeWidth: 1.5}
              encoding:
                x: {field: timestamp, type: quantitative, title: "t [s]"}
                y: {field: value, type: quantitative, title: pose}
                color: {field: series, type: nominal, legend: {title: null, orient: top}}
    # The same three views the Gazebo half declares, so both backends are read identically.
    explorer:
      notebooks:
      - Analysis:
          run: analysis/analysis_run.ipynb
          config: analysis/analysis_config.ipynb
          campaign: analysis/analysis_campaign.ipynb
execution:
  containers:
    # `roqsim` is an entry point in the `robovast.simulators` group, so it resolves wherever
    # the campaign is composed. Nothing about the simulator travels with the campaign.
    simulation:
      backend: roqsim
      # A path, because roqsim_nav2_example registers no `roqsim.worlds` provider. This is the
      # campaign-wide DEFAULT; a configuration may resolve its own through `sim:`.
      config: world/depot_nav2.yaml
      # No `image:`: the backend supplies the family member, and which registry it comes from
      # is the deployment's business (ROBOVAST_PROJECT, --image-project), not this file's.
      resources:
        # Request the sustained figure, cap generously: the simulator is not under test and
        # its startup burst compiles depot's geometry, so no single honest number exists.
        cpu: 0.5
        cpu_limit: 6
        memory: 2944Mi
    # The system under test, kept vanilla: a nav2 image plus the TB4 description and nothing
    # of ours. It must name an image -- RoboVAST has no default for the thing under test.
    sut:
      image: family:robovast
      system_packages:
      # The description nav2_bringup's tb4_simulation_launch.py uses, so both halves publish
      # an identical TF tree. files/nav2_sut.launch.py feeds it to robot_state_publisher.
      - ros-jazzy-nav2-minimal-tb4-description
      # What the `sut:` channel addresses. Declaring the file is what lets a destination be
      # checked against it before the campaign spends anything.
      config_files:
        nav2: files/nav2_params.yaml
      resources:
        # No cpu_limit split: an allocation the container never reaches cannot have shaped
        # the run, and run_validity_view says per run whether the ceiling bound it.
        cpu: 3
        memory: 640Mi
    scenario:
      resources:
        # Same reasoning as the simulator: scenario-execution is not under test.
        cpu: 0.75
        cpu_limit: 2
        memory: 896Mi
  mode: ros2
  runs: 10
  # Without it `stalled` is null and a wedged run looks like one still working. Batch mode,
  # so progress never legitimately pauses mid-run; this is a backstop with room.
  timeout: 300
  scenario_file: scenario_roqsim.osc
  run_files:
  # Per-extension rather than "files/*": that glob crosses '/' and swept __pycache__, which
  # is hashed into the config identity. These reach the SUT too.
  - "files/*.yaml"
  - "files/*.pgm"
  - "files/*.xml"
  - "files/*.sdf"
  - "files/*.py"
  env:
  - PYTHONUNBUFFERED: "1"
results_processing:
  # Declared rather than discovered: a check that ran everywhere would write "ok" for every
  # run of a non-nav2 stack -- a clean bill for a stack that was never there.
  health_checks:
  - nav2_control_loop_rate
  postprocessing:
  # `all` extracts every TF child frame resolving against map, so a world that later gains a
  # walker shows it. `require` names what the analysis is measured from; a run missing one fails.
  - rosbags_tf_to_csv:
      frames: all
      require: [base_link, odom, turtlebot4_base_link_gt]
  - rosbags_nav2bt_to_csv
  - nav2_bt_tree: {bt_xml: files/nav2_bt.xml}
  - rosbags_costmap_to_csv:
      topics: [/map, /global_costmap/costmap, /local_costmap/costmap]
